Feature (DONE): Keyboard shortcuts for host and port actions
  As a user
  I want to use keyboard shortcuts
  So that I can quickly interact with hosts and ports in the TUI

  Scenario: Rescanning a host
    Given the user is in the Hosts View
    And the host "192.168.1.1" is selected
    When the user presses "R" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the app should re-scan only that host
    And update the open ports and services in the table

  Scenario: Opening a service in the browser
    Given the user is in the Ports View for host "192.168.1.1"
    And the port 80 (HTTP) is selected
    When the user presses "O" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the system browser should open "http://192.168.1.1"

  Scenario: Copying IP and port to clipboard
    Given the user is in the Ports View for host "192.168.1.1"
    And the port 443 (HTTPS) is selected
    When the user presses "C" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the clipboard should contain "192.168.1.1:443"

Feature (DONE): Service fingerprinting
  As a user
  I want to identify what software and versions are running on each open port
  So that I can understand my network’s services more precisely

  Scenario: Displaying a service fingerprint
    Given the scan has detected port 80 open on "192.168.1.1"
    Then the Ports View should display "nginx 1.25.3" instead of "HTTP"
    And the fingerprint data should be stored in the scan results

Feature (DONE): Hostname and reverse DNS lookup
  As a user
  I want to see the DNS or mDNS hostname next to each IP
  So that I can identify devices more easily

  Scenario: Displaying resolved hostnames
    Given the user has completed a network scan
    When a hostname can be resolved for "192.168.1.1"
    Then the Hosts View should show "192.168.1.1 (router.local)"

  Scenario: Manual DNS resolution
    Given the user is in the Hosts View
    When the user presses "D"
    Then unresolved hosts should attempt reverse DNS lookup
    And the table should refresh with available hostnames

Feature (DONE): Exporting scan results
  As a user
  I want to export my scan results to files
  So that I can review or share them later

  Scenario: Exporting to JSON
    Given a scan is complete
    When the user presses "E" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the app should prompt for export format
    And if the user selects JSON
    Then a file "scan_results.json" should be saved in the export directory

  Scenario: Exporting to CSV
    Given a scan is complete
    When the user chooses CSV export
    Then the file "scan_results.csv" should contain the host, ports, and services

  Scenario: Exporting to Markdown
    Given a scan is complete
    When the user chooses Markdown export
    Then the file "scan_results.md" should be created with formatted tables


Feature: MAC address and vendor resolution
  As a user
  I want to see the MAC address and device vendor for each host
  So that I can identify hardware manufacturers

  Scenario: Viewing MAC and vendor information
    Given the user has completed a scan
    When the user presses "M" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the app should perform an ARP table lookup
    And display columns "MAC Address" and "Vendor" in the Hosts View
    And if a vendor is known, display its name (e.g. "Apple", "TP-Link")

Feature: Graphical network map
  As a user
  I want to view a visual representation of the network topology
  So that I can understand how devices are connected

  Scenario: Opening the network map
    Given the user has completed a scan
    When the user presses "G" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the app should switch to the "Network Map" view
    And display hosts connected to the router in a tree or ASCII graph layout

  Scenario: Exporting the network map
    Given the user is viewing the network map
    When the user presses "E" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the app should generate "network_map.dot" in Graphviz format
    And optionally render a PNG if graphviz is installed

Feature: Scan history and comparison
  As a user
  I want to compare scans over time
  So that I can see what changed in my network

  Scenario: Viewing history
    Given multiple past scans are stored in the history directory
    When the user presses "H" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the app should open the History View
    And display previous scan dates and summary changes

  Scenario: Viewing differences between scans
    Given the user is in the History View
    When the user selects a date and presses Enter
    Then a diff should be displayed showing new and removed hosts
    And changed ports should be highlighted with "added" or "removed"

  Scenario: Deleting old scans
    Given the user is in the History View
    And an old snapshot is selected
    When the user presses "D" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the selected scan history file should be deleted

Feature: View navigation
  As a user
  I want to navigate between views with the keyboard
  So that I can quickly switch contexts without the mouse

  Scenario: Switching between views
    Given the user is in the Hosts View
    When the user presses Enter
    Then the app switches to the Ports View

    When the user presses Left Arrow
    Then the app returns to the Hosts View

    When the user presses "G" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the app switches to the Network Map

    When the user presses "H" (or equivalent key in the settings)
    Then the app switches to the History View


Feature: Configuration and keybinding management
  As a user
  I want to edit and reload configuration files
  So that I can customize shortcuts and behavior without recompiling the app

  Scenario: Editing config files
    Given the user has configuration files in "~/.config/cnet/"
    When the user edits "config.toml" or "keybinds.toml"
    Then the app should load custom settings on next startup

  Scenario: Reloading configuration at runtime
    Given the app is running
    When the user presses "Ctrl+R" (or equivalent)
    Then all settings and keybindings should reload from "~/.config/cnet/"
    And the app should show a toast "Configuration reloaded successfully"

  Scenario: Restoring default settings
    Given the user is in the Settings View
    When the user presses "D"
    Then all configurations should reset to default values
    And overwrite "keybinds.toml" and "config.toml" with defaults

  Scenario: Showing current keybindings
    Given the user presses "?"
    Then an overlay should appear listing all current shortcuts
    And show their current assignments from the config file


Feature: Settings view
  As a user
  I want to modify runtime parameters from inside the TUI
  So that I can fine-tune the app without editing config files manually

  Scenario: Opening the settings view
    Given the user presses "S" (or equivalent)
    Then the app switches to the Settings View
    And shows adjustable fields such as "Scan timeout", "Export format", and "Default interface"

  Scenario: Changing a setting
    Given the user is in the Settings View
    When they adjust the "Scan timeout" value
    And press Save
    Then the new value should take effect immediately
    And persist in the configuration file

  Scenario: Canceling a change
    Given the user is in the Settings View
    When they make a change and press Cancel
    Then the app should not modify any configuration
